,title,link,text,language,family
0,Bayono–Awbono languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bayono–Awbono_languages,"However, according to Dryer (2022), based on a preliminary quantitative analysis of data from the ASJP database, Bayono–Awbono is likely to be a subgroup of Trans–New Guinea.",Kopkaka,Trans-New Guinea
1,Kopka language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kopka_language,"Kopkaka (Kopka) is an Ok language spoken in Seredala District, Yahukimo Regency, Highland Papua. The villages that speak this language are Seredala, Moruf, Mosomduba, Burupmakot, and others.",Kopkaka,Trans-New Guinea
2,Trans–New Guinea languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trans–New_Guinea_languages,"Trans–New Guinea is an extensive family of Papuan languages spoken on the island of New Guinea and neighboring islands, a region corresponding to the country Papua New Guinea as well as parts of Indonesia.",Kopkaka,Trans-New Guinea
3,Kopkaka [Newguineaworld],https://newguineaworld.linguistik.uzh.ch/families/trans-new-guinea/central-west-new-guinea/digul-river-ok/ok/west-ok/kopkaka,"Kroneman (n.d.) unpublished survey vocabulary of Kopkaka of Unokob village, obtained in digitalized form from Paul Whitehouse via the Summer Institute of Linguistics.",Kopkaka,Trans-New Guinea
4,"Kopkaka Language (OPK) – L1 & L2 Speakers, Status, Map ...",https://www.ethnologue.com/language/opk/,"Kopkaka is an endangered indigenous language of Indonesia. It belongs to the Trans-New Guinea language family. Direct evidence is lacking, but the language is thought to be used as a first language by a decreasing number of young people. It is not known to be taught in schools.",Kopkaka,Trans-New Guinea
5,Papua New Guinea Traditional Songs (1 Hour Compilation ...TransNewGuinea.orgKopkaka - opk (Trans-New Guinea) - PeopleGroups.org,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z05H4u8ZXNY,"Sep 19, 2025 · Papua New Guinea Traditional Songs (1 Hour Compilation) Tribal Music - Mamuna (Hypnotic) Perfect for: deep focus/work, mindful walking, meditation, breathwork, study, reading, nature... The Trans-New Guinea language family currently occupies most of the interior of New Guinea. This family is possibly the third largest in the world with 400 languages and is tentatively thought to have originated with root-crop agriculture around 10,000 years ago. Kopkaka (opk) is part of the Trans-New Guinea family and Trans-New Guinea, Ok-Awyu, Ok, Western class.",Kopkaka,Trans-New Guinea
6,TransNewGuinea.org,https://www.transnewguinea.org/,"The Trans-New Guinea language family currently occupies most of the interior of New Guinea. This family is possibly the third largest in the world with 400 languages and is tentatively thought to have originated with root-crop agriculture around 10,000 years ago.",Kopkaka,Trans-New Guinea
7,Kopkaka - opk (Trans-New Guinea) - PeopleGroups.org,https://www.peoplegroups.org/language/opk/,"Kopkaka (opk) is part of the Trans-New Guinea family and Trans-New Guinea, Ok-Awyu, Ok, Western class.",Kopkaka,Trans-New Guinea
8,Kopkaka | Ethnologue Free,https://www.ethnologue.com/language/opk/?cf=1&token=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9..5hCkgvICFbZIe7hyPnJuA3lq0mLl-LoUMPzkWbPkuio,"Kopkaka is an endangered indigenous language of Indonesia. It belongs to the Trans-New Guinea language family. Direct evidence is lacking, but the language is thought to be used as a first language by a decreasing number of young people.",Kopkaka,Trans-New Guinea
9,Kopkaka - opk (Trans-New Guinea) - PeopleGroups.org,https://peoplegroups.org/language/opk/,"Kopkaka – opk is in the Trans-New Guinea family, included in Trans-New Guinea, Ok-Awyu, Ok, Western language class.",Kopkaka,Trans-New Guinea
